 ### Volume Rendering with Mipmapping
-Applies mipmapping to the retained texture to improve rendering performance in a hybrid CPU-GPU pipeline [S4, unofficial]. The process involves uploading the final 3D texture atlas and lookup tables to the GPU, then rendering the volume slice-by-slice by drawing a proxy polygon for each slice. A fragment shader samples the correct texels from the packed texture using the lookup tables, selecting the appropriate mipmap level based on distance to reduce aliasing and improve speed [S4, unofficial].
+Applies mipmapping to the retained texture to improve rendering performance in a hybrid CPU-GPU pipeline [S4, unofficial]. The process involves uploading the final 3D texture atlas and lookup tables to the GPU, then rendering the volume slice-by-slice by drawing a proxy polygon for each slice [S4, unofficial]. A fragment shader samples the correct texels from the packed texture using the lookup tables, selecting the appropriate mipmap level based on distance to reduce aliasing and improve speed [S4, unofficial].
 
 ### WebGPU Volume Mipmap Generation
-Automatic generation of mipmap chains for volume textures in WebGPU, performed after data upload and after rendering to slices. The shader is created lazily on first use [S5, unofficial]. For unfilterable formats (e.g., 32-bit float formats on devices without the `float32-filterable` capability), it generates mips using a samplerless fallback that reads texels with `textureLoad` and computes the average of the previous mip level's texels [S5, unofficial]. The unfilterable path uses `textureLoad` (no sampler) while the filterable path uses standard `textureSample`. They share a shader per dimension, selected by the `UNFILTERABLE` define [S5, unofficial]. A known pitfall is that if a source dimension is odd, the 2x2x2 load block shifts, causing spatial bias that affects float textures specifically [S5, unofficial].
